\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The modelling object, and where every mechanism we know of sits in it.} A multivariate relation is carried by \emph  {both} margins, and on each margin it takes \emph  {two} predicted quantities: a \textbf  {capacity} --- how much influence the node may commit or absorb --- and a \textbf  {shape} --- how that influence is distributed over its incident edges, including which edges are \emph  {exactly} zero. Capacity without shape bounds a total but does not decide membership; shape without capacity redistributes a total the mechanism does not control. \emph  {Exclusivity is the pair, and no prior mechanism holds both cells on either margin.} ``learned$(k)$'' means a capacity predicted from the participant alone; ``inherited'' means taken from what an unmodified layer allocates, which is what makes an exclusive margin commensurate with a non-exclusive one (Prop.~\ref {prop:twostep}). \textbf  {MarSea claims the two shape cells and inherits the two capacities}; MESH is its mirror image, learning both capacities and predicting neither shape --- and only the shape cells produce the exact zeros that Prop.~\ref {prop:fullsupportceiling} keys on, which is why every ``dense'' row is pinned at $m_j/n_q$ however well its capacities are learned. $\dagger $ marks a mechanism that runs on a declared \emph  {part} of a margin rather than all of it.}}{3}{table.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:object}{{1}{3}{\textbf {The modelling object, and where every mechanism we know of sits in it.} A multivariate relation is carried by \emph {both} margins, and on each margin it takes \emph {two} predicted quantities: a \textbf {capacity} --- how much influence the node may commit or absorb --- and a \textbf {shape} --- how that influence is distributed over its incident edges, including which edges are \emph {exactly} zero. Capacity without shape bounds a total but does not decide membership; shape without capacity redistributes a total the mechanism does not control. \emph {Exclusivity is the pair, and no prior mechanism holds both cells on either margin.} ``learned$(k)$'' means a capacity predicted from the participant alone; ``inherited'' means taken from what an unmodified layer allocates, which is what makes an exclusive margin commensurate with a non-exclusive one (Prop.~\ref {prop:twostep}). \textbf {MarSea claims the two shape cells and inherits the two capacities}; MESH is its mirror image, learning both capacities and predicting neither shape --- and only the shape cells produce the exact zeros that Prop.~\ref {prop:fullsupportceiling} keys on, which is why every ``dense'' row is pinned at $m_j/n_q$ however well its capacities are learned. $\dagger $ marks a mechanism that runs on a declared \emph {part} of a margin rather than all of it}{table.1}{}}
