\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {E1, the replication probe.} \textbf  {(a)} The true relation size is held at $m_j=4$ and the candidate set $|\mathcal  {E}_{\cdot j}|$ grows from $4$ to $256$; $12$ seeds, $\tau _j$ taken inside Thm.~\ref {thm:recovery}'s interval. \textbf  {The measured quantity is degree, not mass}: by equation~\ref {eq:step1} the mass is inherited from an unmodified layer, so a mass sweep would show MarSea tracking softmax and would be the wrong instrument. Solid, left axis: fan-out degree. Any full-support scheme reaches every candidate (slope $+1$); MarSea holds $4.00$ throughout (slope $-0.0000$), which is Cor.~\ref {cor:capacity}'s cardinality half. Dashed, right axis: precision, which for the comparator is $P_j=m_j/|\mathcal  {E}_{\cdot j}|$ at \emph  {every} temperature and scoring function (Prop.~\ref {prop:fullsupportceiling}) --- an identity, not a tendency --- and $1.000$ for MarSea. \textbf  {(b)} The one link that is not an identity. With the capacity inherited, $\tau _j$ carries the whole fan-out claim and must place $k^\star $ at $m_j$; its head reads $\nu _j=\|p_{\cdot j}\|_2^{-2}$, and whether $\nu _j$ tracks $m_j$ depends on $\tau _j$ and the column. Six keys, true sizes $1$--$32$ at $n_q=63$, error bars one standard deviation. Under a per-key $\tau _j$ inside its interval it tracks; under one global $\tau $ it saturates, which is Cor.~\ref {cor:necessity} in training-free form. Both panels are training-free possibility results. Reproduced by \texttt  {v1\_probe.py}.}}{13}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:probe}{{2}{13}{\textbf {E1, the replication probe.} \textbf {(a)} The true relation size is held at $m_j=4$ and the candidate set $|\mathcal {E}_{\cdot j}|$ grows from $4$ to $256$; $12$ seeds, $\tau _j$ taken inside Thm.~\ref {thm:recovery}'s interval. \textbf {The measured quantity is degree, not mass}: by \eqref {eq:step1} the mass is inherited from an unmodified layer, so a mass sweep would show MarSea tracking softmax and would be the wrong instrument. Solid, left axis: fan-out degree. Any full-support scheme reaches every candidate (slope $+1$); MarSea holds $4.00$ throughout (slope $-0.0000$), which is Cor.~\ref {cor:capacity}'s cardinality half. Dashed, right axis: precision, which for the comparator is $P_j=m_j/|\mathcal {E}_{\cdot j}|$ at \emph {every} temperature and scoring function (Prop.~\ref {prop:fullsupportceiling}) --- an identity, not a tendency --- and $1.000$ for MarSea. \textbf {(b)} The one link that is not an identity. With the capacity inherited, $\tau _j$ carries the whole fan-out claim and must place $k^\star $ at $m_j$; its head reads $\nu _j=\|p_{\cdot j}\|_2^{-2}$, and whether $\nu _j$ tracks $m_j$ depends on $\tau _j$ and the column. Six keys, true sizes $1$--$32$ at $n_q=63$, error bars one standard deviation. Under a per-key $\tau _j$ inside its interval it tracks; under one global $\tau $ it saturates, which is Cor.~\ref {cor:necessity} in training-free form. Both panels are training-free possibility results. Reproduced by \texttt {v1\_probe.py}}{figure.2}{}}
