\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Restriction to a scope, checked. Zero violations throughout, with $^{\dagger }$ as in Table~\ref {tab:verification}. The fourth row is run once rather than at each $n_q$: the scope recovery check does not depend on $n_q$ --- the pool outside the scope never enters it --- so repeating it six times would report one result as six. \textbf  {That $n_q$-invariance is the appendix's point and it is structural, not empirical}; what varies with $n_q$ is the \emph  {full-column} comparison, which is Table~\ref {tab:margin}. The last row confirms a \emph  {negative} --- that the $n_q/n_k$ bound is escaped once mass may sit off $\mathcal  {E}$; that it is escaped at all is arranged by the mask density the generator draws, so the row shows the bound is not automatic, not how far it falls. The harness separately prints the rate at which a revocable scope breaks permanence of exclusion, which the fifth row's hypothesis excludes.}}{28}{table.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:transferchecks}{{6}{28}{Restriction to a scope, checked. Zero violations throughout, with $^{\dagger }$ as in Table~\ref {tab:verification}. The fourth row is run once rather than at each $n_q$: the scope recovery check does not depend on $n_q$ --- the pool outside the scope never enters it --- so repeating it six times would report one result as six. \textbf {That $n_q$-invariance is the appendix's point and it is structural, not empirical}; what varies with $n_q$ is the \emph {full-column} comparison, which is Table~\ref {tab:margin}. The last row confirms a \emph {negative} --- that the $n_q/n_k$ bound is escaped once mass may sit off $\mathcal {E}$; that it is escaped at all is arranged by the mask density the generator draws, so the row shows the bound is not automatic, not how far it falls. The harness separately prints the rate at which a revocable scope breaks permanence of exclusion, which the fifth row's hypothesis excludes}{table.6}{}}
